Volume = {28},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {Water scarcity, rapid urbanization, and regional disparities challenge
   the coordinated development of ecological and socio-economic systems in
   global river basins. Focusing on Yellow River Basin, this study
   investigates how ``new quality productive forces{''}-driven by
   innovation, digitalization, low-carbon transitions, and green growth-can
   foster synergies between ecological protection and socio-economic
   progress through historical assessment, spatiotemporal analysis
   (2000-2020), and comparative case studies. Findings reveal a governance
   shift from flood control toward integrated management that links
   ecological restoration, industrial upgrading, and rural revitalization.
   Concurrent ecological and economic gains are evident: forest, grassland,
   and water body coverage increased (9.76 \%-{*}11.41 \%, 56.86
   \%-{*}57.21 \%, and 0.59 \%-{*}0.79 \%, respectively), impervious
   surfaces nearly doubled (1.71 \%-{*}3.06 \%), and Net Primary
   Productivity (NPP) improved, with low-NPP areas declining from 55.14 \%
   to 44.88 \% and high-NPP areas more than doubling to 10.40 \%. However,
   persistent regional disparities remain-over 85 \% of the basin is
   sparsely populated, and 90 \% falls within low-GDP categories. Drawing
   on domestic and international insights, we propose spatially
   differentiated strategies: water-efficient agriculture in the lower
   reaches, sustainable urban-industrial transformation in the middle
   reaches, and large-scale ecological restoration with renewable energy
   deployment in the upper reaches. Policy recommendations emphasize
   digital technologies, ecological compensation, and basin-wide governance
   to advance resource efficiency, decarbonization, and equitable
   development. This study provides actionable pathways for sustainable
   transformation in the Yellow River Basin and analogous socio-ecological
   systems worldwide.},
